  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nRegional radar imagery shows an expansive area of showers \nongoing across the region early this morning, with the earlier \nfocused axis of torrential rainfall in eastern Nebraska having \nsince scattered out for the most part. A broader area of steady\nrainfall, with embedded higher rainfall rates, is beginning to \nset up across northeast Iowa, far northwest Illinois, into \nsouthwest Wisconsin initially this morning. This favors mainly \nlighter rain and somewhat intermittent coverage for much of the\narea through the morning before this activity ultimately drifts\nback south into northern Illinois toward mid-late morning.\n\nMeanwhile a separate area of renewed heavier shower development \nwill likely occur in response to the phasing of a disturbance \nlifting northeast from the southern KS/MO border with a separate\ndisturbance moving into southern IA, eventually reaching the\narea toward the afternoon. In contrast to yesterday, there is a\nbrief window when a narrow corridor of weak instability (up to \n250-500 J/kg MLCAPE) may develop in response to this feature as\nit moves through the area into early evening. The presence of \nweak instability and near-record moisture content (2\"+ PWATs) \nwould support higher rainfall rates, potentially locally in \nexcess of 1\"/hr. While guidance has been fairly consistent in \nthe cold front progressing steadily across the area today, any \ntraining of heavier showers and isolated embedded thunderstorms \nmay still yield localized corridor(s) of rainfall amounts in \nexcess of 2\". The threat of flooding would be mainly tied to \nthese narrow corridors of higher amounts. However, confidence in\nif and where these ultimately set up remains low and \naccordingly, plan to hold off on any additional flood-related \nheadlines. Widespread rainfall amounts of 1-2\" will still lead \nto some rises on area rivers and streams.\n\nIn the wake of this evening's cold front, persistent northerly\nwinds and cold air advection will lead to a seasonably cool\nend to the work week on Friday with high temperatures in the \n60s, lowest along the lakeshore.\n\nThe upper level pattern transitions toward a more zonal WNW to\nESE orientation this weekend into next week. Temperatures \nremain near seasonable to seasonably cool with lower and more \ncomfortable humidity. Several low-amplitude waves will move \nwithin the upper jet to our north and swing mainly dry cold \nfronts across the area at times.\n\nPetr",
